Z-index
========
z-index controls the stacking order of overlapping elements.

An element with a higher z-index appears in front.
An element with a lower z-index appears behind.
For example, an element with z-index: 10 appears above one with z-index: 5.
It works mainly with positioned elements using relative, absolute, fixed, or sticky.
If two elements have the same z-index, the element written later in the HTML usually appears on top.

In simple terms, z-index decides which overlapping element is closer to the viewer.


